course: dictionary
lesson: 2759
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which meaning the writer means. Discipline is a good example. Its main meaning is following rules and controlling how you act, but it can also mean punishing someone for breaking rules, or a subject of study.
explain: «Athens loved debate, trade, art and its navy; Sparta loved discipline, obedience and its army.» Obedience sits right next to discipline, so here it means behaving in a controlled, rule-following way. Ask: what meaning fits? Then try it in the sentence to check.
explain: «The Roman army ... won more by discipline, training and teamwork than by having more men.» Training and teamwork are the clues. Discipline here means self-control and working well together, not punishment.
explain: Look for small phrases too. «Self-discipline» means making yourself do what you should, like practising every day. When you see a word you know, check whether it still fits the sentence before you decide what it means.
fact: Discipline can be a noun (you have discipline) or a verb (you discipline someone).
fact: «Discipline» means following rules and controlling how you act.
fact: «Self-discipline» means making yourself do what you should, like practising every day.
fact: To discipline someone means to punish them for breaking rules.
fact: A discipline can also be a subject of study, such as maths or biology.
exercise: «The Roman army won more by discipline, training and teamwork.» What does discipline mean here? | Being well-controlled and following rules as a team | A subject you study at school | Being punished for breaking rules | *Being loud and fast in battle
exercise: The teacher had to discipline the class after they broke the rules. What does discipline mean here? | Win a race | Forget the lesson | Sing a happy song | *Punish someone for breaking rules
exercise: Which of these is a discipline, meaning a subject of study? | Teamwork | Training | *Biology | Obedience
test: «Athens loved debate, trade, art and its navy; Sparta loved discipline, obedience and its army.» In this sentence, what does discipline mean? | *Following rules and controlling how you act | A subject you study | Being a fast runner | Painting pictures
test: «Behaviour management» is jargon for discipline and punishment. What does discipline mean here? | Dancing in a group | Learning a new language | Hiding things from others | *Controlling behaviour and punishing rule-breaking
exam: Maya reads, «She showed great discipline in her training.» Which meaning fits best, and what clue helped you decide? | A subject of study, because the word great is a clue | *Self-control and effort, because the word training is a clue | Being quiet, because nothing else is said | Punishment, because the word showed is a clue
